A||||10||RiceCooker||Timimg Chain Replacement - a Quick ?||||5||
Z||000000||RiceCooker||03-26-2001||02:54 PM||||Does the timing chain /gear need to be replaced after a certain # of miles? My car has 190,000 km (115,000m) and my mechanic is in there anyways since i have a coolant leak coming from the timing cover. (??)<P>I'm pretty sure, but i just need clarification since i dont know too much on the subject.<P>I did use a search and read up on everything i could though.<P>Thanks,<BR>Todd ||||reg||5
Z||000001||RiceCooker||03-26-2001||02:56 PM||||Sorry, it's an 86SE V6 4 spd.<P>would a bad timing chain also cause bad mileage etc etc?<P>Or does it work fine until it lets go?<P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||03-26-2001||03:20 PM||||Well, if it was me, if I had to take the timing cover off for any reason, I'd go ahead and change the timing chain and gears, water pump, and drive belts.  It's such a pain to get to everything over there, if you gotta do it, change as much as you can.  Especially with high mileage.  The Haynes manual says if you have over 25,000 miles on your timing chain, to go ahead and replace it if you have to remove it.  And yes, it can cause bad performance and low economy.  As the chain stretches, it retards the camshaft timing and causes the engine to loose efficiency.||||reg||6
Z||000003||mshill||03-26-2001||03:23 PM||||If you have the waterpump and timing cover off, replace everything you can in there.  With 190K on it you don't want to have to be going in there again until the whole engine lets go.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||RiceCooker||03-26-2001||04:43 PM||||Thanks guys<P>||||reg||14
Z||000005||Voytek||03-27-2001||04:05 PM||||How do you know that the leak is coming from UDERNEATH the timing cover?  It's usually hard to tell on a Fiero because it's pretty tight in there.  Most of the time it will just be your water pump gasket.  That happened to me about 3 years ago.  I couldn't tell exactly where it was coming from so I replaced the water pump gasket (and the pump in the process).  The old one was pretty deteriorated.  The problem was fixed in about 2 hours in the backyard.  Don't let your mechanic bulls**t you.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||03-28-2001||09:43 AM||||My 85SE v-6 4spd developed a leak between the timing chain cover and the block in the thin area near the water passage.  Its not a hard job, just tedious.  With the engine running I could not see exactly where the leak was.  I could only find the leak by using a coolant system pressure tester while the engine was not running.<P>Having the use of a good puller for the harmonic balancer and an impact wrench is a must.<P>I agree, as long as you are that far into the engine you might as well change the chain.  Mine was fairly new when my gasket blew out.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||RiceCooker||03-28-2001||10:59 AM||||Voytek,<P>I can tell you that it's NOT the Water Pump gasket or the pump itself. I replaced it last year and believe me i have been there and back. If it was something i could tackle by myself i would. <BR>And my mechanic is NOT bull$h**ing me. <P>How's the 3.1?<P>------------------<BR>Kids in the back seat cause accidents<BR>Accidents in the back seat cause kids||||reg||1
Z||000008||Chris McDonald||03-28-2001||04:25 PM||||Replace it.  It is easier to do it now than when it leaves stranded.<P>Yes, a slack chain will cause whimpy performance, poor economy, and even some noise.||||reg||1
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||03-28-2001||11:47 PM||||If you weren't going in to change the timing gear (which I think is a good preventive maint. measure) I would say to check the bolts on the water pump and make sure that they were tefloned. If I remember correctly at least two of them go through the water jacket!||||reg||14
Z||000010||RiceCooker||03-29-2001||10:32 AM||||Pickin up the car later today  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>My mechanic told me that the chain was $hit so its a damn good thing i replaced it now. <P>And i'm thinkin that explains my poor mileage too. <P>Thanks eh<BR>Todd||||reg||6
